# Line endings.
#
# The ActionScript row's build output is not a single file: `adt -target cmdline`
# writes a package that is a *directory* -- prog.exe, prog.swf, mimetype,
# META-INF/ and Adobe AIR/ -- and the AIR runtime validates the bytes inside it
# before running the program.  Several of those files are plain text, so with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default, and set on this machine) a checkout
# rewrites them to CRLF and the runtime refuses to start:
#
#   Security warning: invalid license or package - application may have been
#   modified after packaging
#
# Restoring META-INF/AIR/application.xml to the committed bytes is what fixes it;
# the CRLF forms of signatures.xml and of Adobe AIR/.../Licenses/*/COPYING do not
# on their own stop the package from loading.  The whole bundle is therefore
# checked out byte for byte rather than line-ending-converted, so a fresh clone
# runs the row without being repackaged.
#
# The descriptor input needs the same treatment, and for the same reason.  `adt
# -package` does not parse `app.xml` and re-emit it -- it copies it verbatim into
# the bundle as META-INF/AIR/application.xml, byte for byte (verified: the two
# files are identical, 867 bytes, no CRLF).  A line-ending-converted checkout
# would therefore hand `adt` a CRLF descriptor and produce a CRLF
# application.xml inside the package, which is the exact failure above -- and it
# would do so on every rebuild, since `build_all.bat` copies the descriptor from
# `sources/` into each task directory.  Protecting only the packaged output would
# leave the row one rebuild away from breaking, so both copies of the descriptor
# are pinned as well.
#
# Everything else is left to normal text handling: the `.as` sources, the logs
# (`adt.log`, `amxmlc.log`) and the scripts (`build_all.bat`, `verify.py`) are
# recompiled or rewritten on every build and tolerate either convention.
exec/actionscript/*/out/** -text
exec/actionscript/*/app.xml -text
sources/actionscript/app.xml -text
